INT. ABANDONED CURLING RINK - DUSK

A vast barn of a building, its ice long since gone to a grey, cracked sheet. Rust-colored light pours through broken clerestory windows and lies in long bars across the floor. Hanging from the rafters, a faded banner reads PROVINCIAL CHAMPIONS 1987.

TOBIAS WREN (60s, broad shoulders gone soft, a wool cap pulled low) kneels at the hack, one stone beside him. His hands, thick-knuckled and patient, rest on the handle as if on a sleeping animal's flank.

Ten paces away stands his granddaughter, MIREILLE WREN (17, lean, wearing a team jacket two sizes too large). She holds a broom she does not seem to want.

A radio crackles on a rusted bench. A tinny announcer's voice leaks out.

ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
(through radio)
And the final end goes to Saskatoon, who take the national title.

Tobias does not look at the radio. He sets the stone, and pushes. It slides, slow and crooked, and dies far short of the house.

MIREILLE
You pulled it.

TOBIAS
The ice is gone, that's all.

She sweeps a few strokes anyway, out of habit, then stops.

MIREILLE
Coach called. They filled my spot.

Tobias nods, as if to himself. He wipes the handle with his sleeve, slowly, front and back.

TOBIAS
Cold in here.

MIREILLE
Yeah.

Neither moves. Outside, a truck idles, waiting. Its horn gives a single polite tap.

She lowers the broom to the floor, carefully, as one lays down a sleeping child. Then she walks toward the door. At the threshold she turns.

MIREILLE
You coming?

Tobias lifts the stone, studies it, and sets it upright on the bench beside the radio.

TOBIAS
In a minute.

She goes. The light on the floor dims one bar at a time.
